-- title:
--   Fibres of the Legendre j-map: the anharmonic orbit
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field with decidable equality in which $2 \neq 0$, and let $s, t \in K$ with $s \neq 0$, $s \neq 1$, $t \neq 0$, $t \neq 1$. Write $\mathrm{legendreJ}(u) = 2^{8}(u^{2}-u+1)^{3}/\bigl(u^{2}(u-1)^{2}\bigr)$ for the Legendre $j$-invariant of a parameter $u \in K$ (field division, so the value is $0$ at the excluded parameters $u = 0, 1$). The assertion is the equivalence $$\mathrm{legendreJ}(s) = \mathrm{legendreJ}(t) \iff s \in \{\,t,\; 1-t,\; t^{-1},\; 1-t^{-1},\; (1-t)^{-1},\; 1-(1-t)^{-1}\,\},$$ the right-hand side being membership in the indicated six-element `Finset K` (so, as a list, possibly with repetitions when the orbit degenerates). The six parameters listed are the images of $t$ under the group generated by $t \mapsto 1-t$ and $t \mapsto t^{-1}$; in more familiar form they are $t$, $1-t$, $1/t$, $(t-1)/t$, $1/(1-t)$ and $t/(t-1)$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that two Legendre parameters give elliptic curves with the same $j$-invariant exactly when they lie in the same orbit of the anharmonic group $S_3$ acting on $\mathbb{P}^1 \setminus \{0,1,\infty\}$, i.e. that the $\lambda$-line is a degree-six cover of the $j$-line. It is used in the computation of the sum of inverse widths attached to the Deuring polynomial.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_legendreJ_eq_legendreJ_iff.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_LegendreJ

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

open ModularCurve

theorem ModularCurve.legendreJ_eq_legendreJ_iff {K : Type*} [Field K] [DecidableEq K] (h2 : (2 : K) ≠ 0)
    {s t : K} (hs0 : s ≠ 0) (hs1 : s ≠ 1) (ht0 : t ≠ 0) (ht1 : t ≠ 1) :
    legendreJ s = legendreJ t ↔
      s ∈ ({t, 1 - t, t⁻¹, 1 - t⁻¹, (1 - t)⁻¹, 1 - (1 - t)⁻¹} : Finset K) := by sorry
